1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
The simple flip of a switch brings light, heat, power.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:11,000
But capturing the force of a river, controlling a nuclear reaction,

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:17,000
or transforming the light of the sun into electricity is anything but simple.

4
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Next, Power Plants on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:31,012
Electricity.

6
00:00:31,013 --> 00:00:37,340
It makes us masters of our environment, yet most of us take it for granted.

7
00:00:38,480 --> 00:00:43,060
Deprived of it, we get a sense of what life was like more than 150 years ago,

8
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:46,720
before power transformed our society and our lives.

9
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:51,520
In the middle of the 19th century, a day's work was just that,

10
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:55,260
labor that necessarily took place during the sunlit hours.

11
00:00:55,261 --> 00:01:00,620
work itself was back-breaking, manual, mostly unaided by machinery.

12
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:07,120
The arrival of night meant retreat indoors from the dangers associated with the pervasive darkness.

13
00:01:08,560 --> 00:01:15,120
Over the next century and a half, we took a world which had dominated us and transformed it into this.

14
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:20,080
An electrified environment which responds to our every need.

15
00:01:20,081 --> 00:01:30,567
We control our climate, process information, and tend to our health with power that flows hundreds of miles across an interconnected transmission grid that encompasses

16
00:01:30,568 --> 00:01:31,760
the entire country.

17
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:41,560
Electric current, the flow of charged electrons, intimately influences how we live, yet most of us understand little about how it works.

18
00:01:43,140 --> 00:01:49,520
In 600 BC, the Greeks first discovered that static electricity could be generated by rubbing amber.

19
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:57,480
It wasn't until the 18th century, however, that Benjamin Franklin theorized that electrical fluid might be composed of particles.

20
00:01:58,080 --> 00:02:08,461
By harnessing this flow of electrons or current, inventors and industrialists more than a century later laid the foundation for what would become the colossus of

21
00:02:08,462 --> 00:02:09,880
electrical generation.

22
00:02:10,860 --> 00:02:12,940
The modern power plant.

23
00:02:12,941 --> 00:02:22,340
It can be anything from a red-hot behemoth that once started, consumes trainloads of coal without interruption for years.

24
00:02:23,040 --> 00:02:28,600
Or it might be a nuclear plant, incongruously perched at the edge of the shimmering Pacific Ocean.

25
00:02:29,340 --> 00:02:34,300
A plant that on its own produces 20% of Southern California's energy.

26
00:02:34,301 --> 00:02:41,740
Or maybe it is simply this, a house that generates more energy than it consumes and returns that

27
00:02:41,741 --> 00:02:47,240
surplus to the electrical grid for public use, a new model for the future of power plants.

28
00:02:47,241 --> 00:02:54,160
But whatever, guys, contemporary power plants take, the basics of their design and their

29
00:02:54,161 --> 00:02:59,240
integration into commercial and domestic life were forged more than a hundred years ago

30
00:02:59,241 --> 00:03:05,580
in a conflict between two giants of industry and invention, Thomas Edison and George Westinghouse.

31
00:03:06,220 --> 00:03:13,680
The outcome of the fierce competition between these two men would ultimately dictate how electricity would be generated and transmitted.

32
00:03:14,380 --> 00:03:19,340
But their first battle would be waged over how to bring safer light to the cities of America.

33
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:25,140
Early natural gas lighting systems in homes, for example, on occasion were quite dangerous.

34
00:03:25,141 --> 00:03:32,320
They had no shut-off valves, so if the lamp went out, the gas would continue to accumulate in the home,

35
00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:34,660
and they had some major explosions in homes.

36
00:03:35,080 --> 00:03:40,760
In 1879, Thomas Edison invented the first commercially practical incandescent lamp

37
00:03:40,761 --> 00:03:44,720
with a high-resistance filament that glowed when heated by a low current.

38
00:03:45,580 --> 00:03:50,420
He soon followed this with designs for a complete distribution system for light and power.

39
00:03:50,421 --> 00:04:03,369
On September 4, 1882, after delays and cost overruns, Edison opened the first electric utility, the Pearl Street Station in the heart of Lower Manhattan's financial

40
00:04:03,370 --> 00:04:04,080
district.

41
00:04:05,080 --> 00:04:09,320
Edison knew that his product was going to be expensive and would need to reach many customers.

42
00:04:09,321 --> 00:04:15,940
The whole reason for Edison's Pearl Street station in New York City in 1882

43
00:04:15,941 --> 00:04:22,280
was to demonstrate that electric technology could be used on a widespread basis,

44
00:04:22,460 --> 00:04:27,900
not serving one customer like steam engines in old factories, but many, many customers.

45
00:04:28,660 --> 00:04:34,160
Edison's choice of direct current technology at Pearl Street limited his power plant's range.

46
00:04:34,960 --> 00:04:40,820
The major disadvantage to direct current power and power plants at the time

47
00:04:40,821 --> 00:04:47,660
was you could not transmit electric power very far without losing a tremendous amount of the current.

48
00:04:47,661 --> 00:04:52,600
So you needed a power plant, you know, every half mile or mile throughout the city.

49
00:04:53,060 --> 00:04:59,160
Edison's main competitor, George Westinghouse, saw that electricity's future was in long-distance transmission.

50
00:05:00,140 --> 00:05:02,600
Westinghouse put his company, Westinghouse Electric,

51
00:05:02,940 --> 00:05:06,380
to work at perfecting a system using alternating current,

52
00:05:06,381 --> 00:05:09,760
which flows in reversing waves which propagate more easily

53
00:05:09,761 --> 00:05:12,720
and with less resistance in a transmission wire.

54
00:05:13,440 --> 00:05:18,600
The beauty of alternating current is that you can generate that at a very high voltage

55
00:05:18,601 --> 00:05:22,360
and then step it down essentially to any voltage that you need.

56
00:05:22,920 --> 00:05:28,540
So you could step it down to a very low voltage that somebody would need at a house for their lighting,

57
00:05:28,541 --> 00:05:35,040
or you could step it down to a somewhat higher voltage that some business might need or some factory would need.

58
00:05:35,740 --> 00:05:43,040
George Westinghouse purchased several vital alternating current patents from Nikola Tesla, a Serbian-born inventor.

59
00:05:44,060 --> 00:05:49,120
Westinghouse recognized Tesla's genius and brought the inventor to Pittsburgh to work for him.

60
00:05:50,500 --> 00:05:54,960
Together, they designed the components necessary to alternating currents' success.

61
00:05:55,560 --> 00:06:00,800
In 1887, George Westinghouse invented a meter for alternating current.

62
00:06:00,801 --> 00:06:04,760
He thought if you're going to make alternating current commercially available,

63
00:06:05,240 --> 00:06:08,740
you're going to have to have some way of measuring what you're going to sell to your customers.

64
00:06:10,080 --> 00:06:15,380
Westinghouse's aggressive pursuit of alternating current technology put him in direct conflict with Edison.

65
00:06:16,080 --> 00:06:22,060
It was a battle of business and technological giants, Thomas Edison versus George Westinghouse.

66
00:06:22,820 --> 00:06:25,180
This is called the battle of the currents,

67
00:06:25,480 --> 00:06:28,500
and it was essential because the technology that won

68
00:06:28,501 --> 00:06:33,500
would dominate the industry for the remainder of the foreseeable future.

69
00:06:34,160 --> 00:06:35,780
With so much at stake,

70
00:06:36,060 --> 00:06:39,520
it's not surprising that the battle of the currents took a bitter turn.

71
00:06:39,980 --> 00:06:42,720
Edison went so far as to lobby New York state officials

72
00:06:42,721 --> 00:06:45,400
to have his competitor's product, alternating current,

73
00:06:45,880 --> 00:06:48,820
used to execute condemned men at Sing Sing Prison.

74
00:06:49,440 --> 00:06:51,920
I guess perhaps the lowest blow is when they were looking for

75
00:06:51,921 --> 00:06:53,483
a word to describe this.

76
00:06:53,484 --> 00:06:57,780
Today we say a person is electrocuted, but at the time they didn't

77
00:06:57,781 --> 00:07:03,920
have a term, and Thomas Edison suggested that they use the word Westinghouse, that the condemned

78
00:07:03,921 --> 00:07:05,190
man was Westinghouse.

79
00:07:05,191 --> 00:07:09,000
And obviously it's very, very upsetting to George Westinghouse.

80
00:07:09,480 --> 00:07:13,940
But Westinghouse focused on the industrial competition at hand and left the courting

81
00:07:13,941 --> 00:07:15,840
of public opinion to his adversary.

82
00:07:15,841 --> 00:07:21,520
It was a great World's Fair coming up in 1893 in Chicago,

83
00:07:21,780 --> 00:07:25,960
and they asked for proposals to illuminate the fair, to light it at night,

84
00:07:26,620 --> 00:07:31,320
realizing that nowhere in the world had a major event ever been illuminated at night before.

85
00:07:32,140 --> 00:07:37,960
Westinghouse bid $500,000, half of what Edison bid, and was awarded the contract.

86
00:07:38,380 --> 00:07:41,240
He took advantage of the opportunity to promote his product.

87
00:07:41,241 --> 00:07:47,380
So he installed a complete alternating current power plant that was on display for everyone to see.

88
00:07:47,680 --> 00:07:51,280
It had a giant Westinghouse alternating current switchboard.

89
00:07:51,920 --> 00:07:55,980
And the impressive part of that was that there was only one operator.

90
00:07:56,140 --> 00:08:01,060
One operator could control all the electrical equipment for the World's Fair of 1893.

91
00:08:01,860 --> 00:08:07,480
67 million Americans and foreign visitors attended the Chicago World's Fair of 1893.

92
00:08:08,380 --> 00:08:11,480
Tesla and Westinghouse made sure that they left the fairgrounds

93
00:08:11,481 --> 00:08:14,320
with a good idea of who was winning the battle of the currents.

94
00:08:15,020 --> 00:08:17,940
Both George Westinghouse and Nikola Tesla realized

95
00:08:17,941 --> 00:08:23,260
this was an opportunity to really showcase the potential of alternating current.

96
00:08:23,600 --> 00:08:26,660
One display, they had this huge word, power.

97
00:08:27,440 --> 00:08:30,200
And at night, there were electrical currents,

98
00:08:30,201 --> 00:08:34,780
just like little bolts of lightning flashing all around this illuminated word.

99
00:08:34,781 --> 00:08:39,780
After this dramatic success, Westinghouse was prepared for his greatest challenge,

100
00:08:39,781 --> 00:08:44,780
competing head-to-head with Edison for the right to build the largest power plant in the world.

101
00:08:47,780 --> 00:08:51,780
George Westinghouse built the first illuminated tennis court at his house.

102
00:08:51,781 --> 00:08:55,780
It had 1,500 light bulbs and its own power plant.

103
00:08:55,781 --> 00:08:59,780
Power plants will return on Modern Marvels.

104
00:09:01,780 --> 00:09:03,780
Niagara Falls.

105
00:09:03,781 --> 00:09:12,140
For hundreds of years, men had understood that the power of falling water could be converted into mechanical energy, the energy to run mills.

106
00:09:12,580 --> 00:09:21,906
But by the 1880s, there was open debate over whether the fall should be harnessed with this old technology or transformed into electricity and transmitted to industry

107
00:09:21,907 --> 00:09:23,760
and individuals hungry for power.

108
00:09:24,420 --> 00:09:29,420
If we look at Niagara Falls and the potential for generating power,

109
00:09:29,540 --> 00:09:32,440
it was obvious to a lot of intelligent men at that period of time

110
00:09:32,441 --> 00:09:36,560
that there was a tremendous amount of power in the water coming down over Niagara Falls.

111
00:09:36,680 --> 00:09:39,600
If it could be harnessed, it could do great things for industry.

112
00:09:39,601 --> 00:09:44,300
The International Niagara Commission solicited plans from leading engineers

113
00:09:44,301 --> 00:09:49,760
to design a plant that could transmit electricity to nearby Buffalo's manufacturing interests.

114
00:09:49,761 --> 00:09:54,760
This was a dramatic opportunity for George Westinghouse's alternating current system.

115
00:09:54,761 --> 00:09:58,760
Ultimately, the decision to choose alternating current,

116
00:09:58,761 --> 00:10:04,760
which could be pushed much further distances over transmission lines from the powerhouse,

117
00:10:04,761 --> 00:10:07,760
won the day for alternating current.

118
00:10:07,761 --> 00:10:15,760
It took from 1892 to 1895 to build the tunnel that would divert the force of the Niagara River to the plant's turbines.

119
00:10:15,761 --> 00:10:24,140
During the summer of 1894, electric cranes eased the 29-ton turbines, the largest built

120
00:10:24,141 --> 00:10:26,820
to date, into the wheel pits.

121
00:10:26,821 --> 00:10:30,780
The turbines were essentially windmill blades that could be driven by the force of Niagara's

122
00:10:30,781 --> 00:10:33,020
water.

123
00:10:33,021 --> 00:10:37,600
When the plant was finished, water flowed through the forebays and into the penstocks,

124
00:10:37,601 --> 00:10:42,820
vertical pipes about 8 feet in diameter, before entering and driving the turbines, which in

125
00:10:42,821 --> 00:10:45,280
turn spun rotors beneath the generators.

126
00:10:45,281 --> 00:10:55,260
Inside the generator, a magnet spun, creating a magnetic field which induced an electric charge in a casing of copper coils, thus producing electricity.

127
00:10:55,261 --> 00:11:03,600
In 1896, the Niagara Power Station began transmitting power 17 miles to Buffalo, New York.

128
00:11:04,140 --> 00:11:08,760
But it wasn't until 1901 at the Pan American Exposition on the Buffalo Fairgrounds

129
00:11:08,761 --> 00:11:15,640
that the general public was able to witness a large-scale display of the wonder of long-distance electrical transmission.

130
00:11:16,680 --> 00:11:19,440
In order to transmit electricity over this distance,

131
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:23,860
transformers at the plant increased voltages to 11,000 volts,

132
00:11:23,861 --> 00:11:28,360
four times the voltage of an 1891 plant that Westinghouse had built.

133
00:11:29,880 --> 00:11:34,620
Inside a transformer, an electric charge passes through one wire coil,

134
00:11:35,020 --> 00:11:38,240
inducing a higher or lower charge in another separate coil.

135
00:11:39,040 --> 00:11:42,840
Voltages multiply at the ratio of the number of turns in the primary coil

136
00:11:42,841 --> 00:11:45,740
to the number of turns in the secondary coil.

137
00:11:46,860 --> 00:11:50,640
What the Westinghouse engineers understood very clearly

138
00:11:50,641 --> 00:11:54,880
was that large power plants, which could produce large amounts of electricity,

139
00:11:55,300 --> 00:11:58,920
which could serve larger numbers of people over a wider area,

140
00:11:59,340 --> 00:12:03,680
needed the use of transformers to increase the pressure of electricity

141
00:12:03,681 --> 00:12:06,620
to be able to push it longer distances.

142
00:12:07,300 --> 00:12:11,060
The Niagara power plant provided not only a technical blueprint,

143
00:12:11,640 --> 00:12:16,180
but also an economic model that would be duplicated time and again over the coming century.

144
00:12:16,840 --> 00:12:18,440
If you build it, they will come.

145
00:12:18,441 --> 00:12:23,840
Heavy industry, in this case, would congregate around a reliable, cheap energy source.

146
00:12:23,841 --> 00:12:32,140
There was a fellow in Pittsburgh named Hall who had an early process for the producing of aluminum using electricity.

147
00:12:32,640 --> 00:12:37,160
So he moved his company from Pittsburgh to Niagara Falls when the power became available.

148
00:12:37,440 --> 00:12:40,900
That company went on to change its name to Aluminum Company of America.

149
00:12:41,460 --> 00:12:44,400
And as we know today, it's the largest aluminum company in the world.

150
00:12:44,401 --> 00:12:51,400
It soon became clear as electricity flowed out of the Niagara power plant that alternating current was here to stay.

151
00:12:51,401 --> 00:12:56,400
After Niagara, even Edison abandoned DC for AC technology.

152
00:12:56,401 --> 00:13:03,400
I think it would be very fair to call George Westinghouse the father of the modern power plant.

153
00:13:03,401 --> 00:13:04,690
He was the catalyst.

154
00:13:04,691 --> 00:13:13,400
He was the person that had the commitment for this 10-year period of time to pull the resources to make alternating current successful.

155
00:13:13,401 --> 00:13:14,741
current successful.

156
00:13:14,742 --> 00:13:19,400
In the ensuing decades, improvements in the electrical engineering

157
00:13:19,401 --> 00:13:24,980
of turbines, generators, and transformers would lead men to dream of a bigger and better source

158
00:13:24,981 --> 00:13:26,067
of water power.

159
00:13:26,068 --> 00:13:31,720
This dream would culminate at Hoover Dam, 40 years after the first electricity

160
00:13:31,721 --> 00:13:33,720
flowed out of Niagara.

161
00:13:33,721 --> 00:13:39,900
At 726 feet in height, weighing in at 6.5 million tons and forming a

162
00:13:39,901 --> 00:13:46,460
150-mile lake, Hoover represented a stunning increase in scale to Niagara and is still

163
00:13:46,461 --> 00:13:49,186
one of the world's largest dams.

164
00:13:49,187 --> 00:13:53,700
Hoover's transmission voltage of 287,000 volts speaks

165
00:13:53,701 --> 00:13:56,690
to the engineering leap that had taken place.

166
00:13:56,691 --> 00:13:59,480
A number of people compare Hoover Dam with

167
00:13:59,481 --> 00:14:01,784
the earlier Niagara Falls installation.

168
00:14:01,785 --> 00:14:04,560
The difference between the Niagara Falls of the

169
00:14:04,561 --> 00:14:12,400
early 1890s and Hoover Dam in the mid-1930s is the improvements in technology over that period of

170
00:14:12,401 --> 00:14:13,101
time.

171
00:14:13,102 --> 00:14:20,380
Larger, much more efficient generators, and most importantly, very high voltage or high

172
00:14:20,381 --> 00:14:22,496
pressure transmission systems.

173
00:14:22,497 --> 00:14:26,800
Whereas people 17 miles away from Niagara Falls could receive

174
00:14:26,801 --> 00:14:29,643
electricity in the early 1890s.

175
00:14:29,644 --> 00:14:34,780
By 1936 and 37, when power was coming out of Hoover Dam,

176
00:14:34,900 --> 00:14:38,815
it was going over 250 miles to Southern California.

177
00:14:38,816 --> 00:14:42,040
In the decades between Niagara and Hoover,

178
00:14:42,700 --> 00:14:46,767
privately owned power companies flourished throughout the country.

179
00:14:46,768 --> 00:14:48,000
It did not take long

180
00:14:48,001 --> 00:14:53,560
for company owners to realize that consolidation was the only way to profit in an industry that

181
00:14:53,561 --> 00:15:00,440
required so much investment to build infrastructure.

182
00:15:00,441 --> 00:15:03,218
Tom McCarter had been the Attorney General of New Jersey.

183
00:15:03,219 --> 00:15:04,680
His idea, which was very novel

184
00:15:04,681 --> 00:15:09,640
at the time, was to basically draw a line between New York City and Philadelphia and

185
00:15:09,641 --> 00:15:15,200
buy up all the municipal light and gas companies and traction companies that existed between

186
00:15:15,201 --> 00:15:16,853
those two major points.

187
00:15:16,854 --> 00:15:21,740
And by doing so, he was able to generate the critical mass necessary

188
00:15:21,741 --> 00:15:27,160
to go out and begin to fund the tremendous infrastructure that was going to be required to build the electric system.

189
00:15:27,780 --> 00:15:31,080
It was also a major step in the growth of the transmission grid.

190
00:15:31,840 --> 00:15:33,660
More customers meant greater revenue.

191
00:15:34,240 --> 00:15:37,820
An expanding utility could afford the expense of running transmission lines,

192
00:15:38,380 --> 00:15:40,800
linking multiple plants into a regional grid.

193
00:15:41,660 --> 00:15:47,860
This system spawned the giant interstate power transmission grids that today cover thousands of square miles.

194
00:15:48,760 --> 00:15:53,760
Improved transformer technology and higher voltages meant electricity could be sent farther and farther.

195
00:15:54,620 --> 00:16:02,460
By 1903, you had over 100,000 volt technology, which meant you could push that electricity 150 miles or more.

196
00:16:03,100 --> 00:16:08,920
By the early 1920s, you had 220 and 230,000 volt technology,

197
00:16:08,921 --> 00:16:15,000
which meant literally anything within a range of about 300 miles of the power plant could receive electricity.

198
00:16:15,880 --> 00:16:19,500
The workhorse of this grid is the transmission substation,

199
00:16:20,080 --> 00:16:21,920
like this one outside Athens, Georgia,

200
00:16:22,160 --> 00:16:25,080
where the job of increasing and decreasing voltages is done.

201
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:30,760
Our network grid system is based on about three main voltages,

202
00:16:31,220 --> 00:16:33,760
and then we go to some lower voltages for shorter routes.

203
00:16:33,761 --> 00:16:40,600
But the network system is basically 500,000 volts, 230,000 volts, and 115,000 volts.

204
00:16:41,300 --> 00:16:44,660
The 500,000 volts is basically like an interstate system.

205
00:16:44,661 --> 00:16:53,480
It travels from state to state, and there's about five, six main arteries across the state.

206
00:16:53,560 --> 00:16:56,640
The 230-kV system is sort of like the U.S. highway system.

207
00:16:57,140 --> 00:17:00,400
There's more routes, but still not the largest number.

208
00:17:00,500 --> 00:17:04,940
There's the largest number of the 115,000, which is more like the state highway system.

209
00:17:04,941 --> 00:17:12,140
carries power from town to town, all over the smaller towns, medium-sized towns,

210
00:17:12,360 --> 00:17:14,880
and to other areas to feed different loads.

211
00:17:16,320 --> 00:17:18,820
Transformers at the substation alter the voltages

212
00:17:18,821 --> 00:17:22,060
so that electricity can be routed where it needs to go.

213
00:17:22,880 --> 00:17:27,660
We're standing in front of the 230 to 115 kV transformer, the big box here.

214
00:17:28,100 --> 00:17:34,120
The transformer converts the 230 kV voltage down to 115,000 volts,

215
00:17:34,121 --> 00:17:40,780
and then supplies power to the 115 bus, which is then shipped out across the 115 kV grid.

216
00:17:41,240 --> 00:17:49,320
Electricity flows along the 115 kilovolt power line and gets stepped down as it nears its destination, private homes.

217
00:17:50,720 --> 00:17:54,560
This is a typical distribution transformer serving a home.

218
00:17:56,060 --> 00:18:00,400
Distribution lines either 12,000 or 25,000 volts.

219
00:18:00,401 --> 00:18:11,000
It stepped down to 120 to 240 volts to feed the house for the normal television, lights, appliances, everyday use.

220
00:18:11,960 --> 00:18:16,820
This kind of everyday use has fueled the demand for power throughout the century,

221
00:18:17,200 --> 00:18:19,780
as consumption and desire for comfort have grown.

222
00:18:20,420 --> 00:18:25,480
To meet society's runaway electrical needs, utilities built their bread and butter plants.

223
00:18:26,100 --> 00:18:31,060
Next, what eats 36,000 tons of coal a day and never needs a rest?

224
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:36,340
In 1984, Brazil built the world's largest hydroelectric plant,

225
00:18:36,740 --> 00:18:40,400
which provides as much energy as 13 nuclear reactors.

226
00:18:41,320 --> 00:18:43,880
Our plants will return on Modern Marvels.

227
00:18:44,780 --> 00:18:48,080
On 2,000 acres in Barstow County, Georgia,

228
00:18:48,081 --> 00:18:52,900
sits the second largest coal-fired plant in the United States, Plant Bowen.

229
00:18:52,901 --> 00:19:00,240
Owned and operated by Georgia Power, the facility produces in 15 seconds all the electricity

230
00:19:00,241 --> 00:19:03,140
a home will use in a year.

231
00:19:03,141 --> 00:19:07,500
With the exception of hydroelectric power in water-rich states, most of the country's

232
00:19:07,501 --> 00:19:11,760
electricity has been generated at coal-fired plants.

233
00:19:11,761 --> 00:19:18,260
In its heyday, coal was as plentiful as environmental concerns were scarce.

234
00:19:18,261 --> 00:19:22,780
flooded into utilities, and coal plants grew bigger and more efficient.

235
00:19:23,360 --> 00:19:28,700
Power plants went from small cottage industry to big, massive power plants.

236
00:19:29,360 --> 00:19:34,520
They were taking this steam cycle and really wringing every ounce of energy from it that they could

237
00:19:34,521 --> 00:19:36,360
and doing so in a very efficient way.

238
00:19:36,900 --> 00:19:40,320
Basically, the way you generate electricity, you take water,

239
00:19:40,321 --> 00:19:44,280
and the simplest cycle is you boil it and you create steam,

240
00:19:44,281 --> 00:19:48,500
and you pressurize it with this amount of heat that you're introducing into it

241
00:19:48,501 --> 00:19:50,100
and you put it through a turbine.

242
00:19:50,760 --> 00:19:52,280
And the turbine is a simple fan

243
00:19:52,281 --> 00:19:56,080
and as you introduce the high-pressure steam, it turns and it turns a generator.

244
00:19:56,760 --> 00:19:59,500
But this simple steam cycle was wasteful.

245
00:19:59,720 --> 00:20:01,600
It would take about 10 pounds of coal

246
00:20:01,601 --> 00:20:05,760
to produce the amount of energy that it would take to light a 100-watt light bulb for an hour.

247
00:20:06,400 --> 00:20:07,860
That's a very significant amount of coal.

248
00:20:08,460 --> 00:20:11,780
Today, through technological changes, we've been able to bring that downward now.

249
00:20:12,200 --> 00:20:13,980
We're far under one pound of coal.

250
00:20:14,280 --> 00:20:16,280
to produce the same amount of energy.

251
00:20:16,281 --> 00:20:21,280
It took a combination of disciplines to affect this kind of fuel efficiency.

252
00:20:21,281 --> 00:20:24,280
You had the power plant engineers refining that process,

253
00:20:24,281 --> 00:20:26,280
increasing the thermal efficiency,

254
00:20:26,281 --> 00:20:30,280
finding out how to use fuels in a more efficient way.

255
00:20:30,281 --> 00:20:34,280
You had metallurgists working on piping that were trying to bring temperatures up higher.

256
00:20:34,281 --> 00:20:40,280
You had boiler feed pump folks working on producing tremendous amounts of pressure.

257
00:20:40,281 --> 00:20:46,380
All this to supply energy to a growing population and industrial base that demanded more power.

258
00:20:47,020 --> 00:20:53,200
Engineers call this demand load, and this load changes depending on the season and time of day.

259
00:20:54,240 --> 00:20:58,200
Load is the electrical demand on our system.

260
00:20:58,840 --> 00:21:02,380
If you turn a light bulb on, that is putting a load on our system.

261
00:21:02,381 --> 00:21:06,220
It's putting a demand for the use of electricity.

262
00:21:06,221 --> 00:21:10,960
The use of power goes up dramatically during the day, particularly today with air conditioning,

263
00:21:11,200 --> 00:21:13,940
so that you have very low levels of load at 8 o'clock in the morning.

264
00:21:14,440 --> 00:21:18,880
It goes up higher and higher in the morning period, and then about 3 o'clock in the afternoon,

265
00:21:19,340 --> 00:21:24,840
everybody's air conditioning is working at its maximum, industry is working hard, and that's the maximum peak load.

266
00:21:25,440 --> 00:21:30,060
To service this energy cycle, a utility needs three types of plants.

267
00:21:30,061 --> 00:21:36,060
You have base load, which is 24-hour load, and that's a plant that can run full-time.

268
00:21:36,220 --> 00:21:39,980
You have intermediate load, which is a plant that will cycle.

269
00:21:40,120 --> 00:21:42,380
It'll come on in the morning, and then at night it'll shut down.

270
00:21:42,780 --> 00:21:47,260
And then you have peaking facilities, which run really for just a few hours a day.

271
00:21:47,800 --> 00:21:50,900
And you need three different kinds of machines to cover those loads.

272
00:21:51,520 --> 00:21:54,680
Plant bone is what we call a base load power plant.

273
00:21:55,280 --> 00:22:01,560
The economics of operating it are such that it pretty well runs day in and day out, 24 hours a day.

274
00:22:01,561 --> 00:22:10,140
It takes four 9,000-ton trainloads of low-sulfur coal arriving from eastern Kentucky each day to supply the plant.

275
00:22:10,700 --> 00:22:17,340
The coal is pulverized to the consistency of talcum powder before being blown into the boilers to be burned to heat water.

276
00:22:18,120 --> 00:22:26,240
It's turned into steam, steam that has a temperature of 1,000 degrees and a pressure of 3,500 pounds per square inch.

277
00:22:26,241 --> 00:22:30,349
This steam is delivered to a turbine. It turns a turbine.

278
00:22:30,350 --> 00:22:33,160
The turbine is attached to a generator.

279
00:22:33,720 --> 00:22:36,849
The generator and the turbine spin at 3,600 RPMs.

280
00:22:36,850 --> 00:22:41,320
That generates electricity, which we put out on our transmission line.

281
00:22:42,220 --> 00:22:52,300
Once the steam has done its work in the turbine, it enters the condenser, where it flows over 36,000 copper tubes containing water from the nearby Etowah River.

282
00:22:52,301 --> 00:23:00,560
The river water absorbs heat, condensing the steam back into water so that it can be reused in the boiler.

283
00:23:01,300 --> 00:23:09,200
Hot river water is then cooled in 400-foot-high cooling towers, each tower cooling 300,000 gallons a minute.

284
00:23:10,600 --> 00:23:19,220
Not surprisingly, it is no longer possible to find communities who will welcome a plant of Bowen scale with its looming 1,000-foot-high stacks.

285
00:23:19,221 --> 00:23:31,080
As a result, utilities like Georgia Power have developed peaking facilities with much smaller footprints, both in terms of land use and environmental impact.

286
00:23:32,300 --> 00:23:42,540
Combustion turbine plants like Plant Dahlberg in northeast Georgia burn natural gas or fuel oil instead of coal in what is essentially a souped-up jet engine.

287
00:23:43,420 --> 00:23:48,420
The gas turbine engine uses hot air and not steam to produce electricity.

288
00:23:49,220 --> 00:23:54,420
Fuel mixes with air, is burned, and the expanding gases drive the turbine.

289
00:23:54,421 --> 00:23:59,720
Once the turbine spins, the process is much the same as in a steam cycle plant.

290
00:23:59,721 --> 00:24:07,220
The technology for this type of unit with the combustion turbine is very similar to what you would see on a jet engine.

291
00:24:07,221 --> 00:24:13,720
The jet engine has a compressor, brings air in, compresses it, burns it, and produces the power they use in thrust.

292
00:24:13,721 --> 00:24:17,720
This is a much larger machine doing the same thing.

293
00:24:17,721 --> 00:24:21,400
It has been equated to a jet engine on steroids.

294
00:24:22,360 --> 00:24:27,100
Plant Dahlberg's eight units combined produce about 600 megawatts of energy,

295
00:24:27,500 --> 00:24:30,420
less than the output of a single unit at Plant Bowen.

296
00:24:31,080 --> 00:24:36,700
As a result, the turbines are only used to produce supplemental electricity when it is needed.

297
00:24:38,240 --> 00:24:43,660
It's used to provide energy to get you through the high demand period of the day.

298
00:24:43,661 --> 00:24:54,200
Today, the energy market and societal pressures for safer, cleaner power have created a demand for smaller plants that are more evenly distributed on the landscape.

299
00:24:55,040 --> 00:25:00,460
This is changing the shape of an industry whose motto had always been, bigger is better.

300
00:25:00,461 --> 00:25:05,580
Built during the years that followed the Depression,

301
00:25:06,560 --> 00:25:08,820
those bigger plants produced more power.

302
00:25:09,240 --> 00:25:13,880
But in the 1930s and 40s, rural farms had no way of tapping into that power.

303
00:25:14,480 --> 00:25:17,080
Remote, expensive to reach with transmission lines,

304
00:25:17,400 --> 00:25:18,940
they simply were not on the grid.

305
00:25:19,560 --> 00:25:23,740
Investor-owned utilities primarily did not want to go to the cost

306
00:25:23,741 --> 00:25:26,980
of providing electricity to rural customers

307
00:25:26,981 --> 00:25:32,420
because you would face the cost of building power lines, in some cases, 100 miles long

308
00:25:32,421 --> 00:25:34,520
that would only serve maybe four or five customers.

309
00:25:34,880 --> 00:25:36,360
No return on your investment.

310
00:25:37,460 --> 00:25:42,760
President Roosevelt and the Congress established the Tennessee Valley Authority in 1933

311
00:25:42,761 --> 00:25:47,020
and the Rural Electrification Administration in 1935.

312
00:25:47,740 --> 00:25:51,660
The agencies shared the goal of bringing electricity to rural America,

313
00:25:52,180 --> 00:25:54,440
where only 10% of farms had power.

314
00:25:54,441 --> 00:26:01,200
The TVA ostensibly began as a flood control effort by the Army Corps of Engineers,

315
00:26:01,201 --> 00:26:09,640
but it was quickly used as a way to find new sources of hydroelectric generation on the rivers of the area.

316
00:26:10,340 --> 00:26:13,900
Dams were built, ultimately totaling 42 in the system,

317
00:26:14,400 --> 00:26:17,420
and a massive hydroelectric power project was launched,

318
00:26:17,760 --> 00:26:22,380
which grew to a total capacity of 4 million kilowatts by the 1970s.

319
00:26:22,381 --> 00:26:27,380
By this time, the mission of the REA and the TVA had been accomplished.

320
00:26:27,381 --> 00:26:33,380
Ninety-eight percent of all farms in the United States had electric service.

321
00:26:33,381 --> 00:26:38,380
Once the TVA had exhausted the hydroelectric possibilities of the river basin,

322
00:26:38,381 --> 00:26:42,380
administrators turned their attention to other forms of power.

323
00:26:42,381 --> 00:26:46,380
In later years, the TVA moved away from its hydroelectric base

324
00:26:46,381 --> 00:26:53,460
base and actually was a pioneer in the scale up of some of the coal-fired boiler technology

325
00:26:53,461 --> 00:27:01,060
that we see today and became a die-hard proponent of nuclear power in the 50s and the 1960s.

326
00:27:01,900 --> 00:27:04,893
Next, the half-life of nuclear power.

327
00:27:04,894 --> 00:27:09,180
How a technology grew up and got old before its time.

328
00:27:09,181 --> 00:27:17,760
The United States has 5% of the world's population, yet consumes 25% of its energy supply.

329
00:27:18,900 --> 00:27:21,620
Power plants will return on Modern Marvels.

330
00:27:22,440 --> 00:27:28,040
In 1942, Enrico Fermi created the first self-sustaining nuclear chain reaction

331
00:27:28,041 --> 00:27:31,720
in a lab hidden beneath the football field at the University of Chicago.

332
00:27:34,780 --> 00:27:37,800
And in doing so, launched the Atomic Age.

333
00:27:39,180 --> 00:27:44,640
In an atom bomb, a sufficient quantity of weapons-grade plutonium is imploded by a TNT charge,

334
00:27:45,080 --> 00:27:47,760
initiating a chain reaction, or fission explosion.

335
00:27:49,360 --> 00:27:58,540
Vision is the process in which natural elements that are radioactive in nature, like uranium,

336
00:27:59,160 --> 00:28:07,000
is bombarded with neutrons, and then by bombarded with neutrons, they split into other elements.

337
00:28:07,001 --> 00:28:10,140
and when they split into other elements, they release energy.

338
00:28:11,200 --> 00:28:14,700
Fortunately, not all nuclear reactions end in devastation.

339
00:28:15,680 --> 00:28:20,700
It was the peacetime harnessing of atomic energy that spawned the nuclear power industry.

340
00:28:21,860 --> 00:28:27,120
The first privately financed and commercial nuclear power plant

341
00:28:27,121 --> 00:28:28,840
that was built without government help

342
00:28:28,841 --> 00:28:33,600
was the Dresden 1 facility outside of Chicago in 1959.

343
00:28:33,601 --> 00:28:39,720
If you think of the Manhattan Project and the atomic bomb and the ending of World War II,

344
00:28:40,660 --> 00:28:47,660
the advancement in that technology over a 10 to 15 year period was pretty dramatic.

345
00:28:48,460 --> 00:28:53,240
Today, more than 100 nuclear power plants are operating in the United States,

346
00:28:53,680 --> 00:28:56,500
supplying 20% of the country's energy needs.

347
00:28:57,860 --> 00:29:00,640
San Onofre is what we call a baseload power plant,

348
00:29:00,641 --> 00:29:08,120
which means it tries to operate 24 hours a day, 365 days a year at full power output,

349
00:29:08,520 --> 00:29:12,620
which in the case of each of these reactors is a little over 1,100 megawatts of power.

350
00:29:13,020 --> 00:29:17,660
The San Onofre Nuclear Generating Station in California's San Diego County

351
00:29:17,661 --> 00:29:21,700
supplies energy for 2.5 million homes and businesses.

352
00:29:22,620 --> 00:29:23,900
Fuel is uranium.

353
00:29:24,320 --> 00:29:27,060
Uranium has the natural ability to fission,

354
00:29:27,061 --> 00:29:31,140
which means that the nucleus of the atom of uranium can literally tear itself apart.

355
00:29:31,680 --> 00:29:34,120
When it does that, it releases a tiny amount of heat,

356
00:29:34,240 --> 00:29:36,680
but what it also releases are additional neutrons.

357
00:29:37,420 --> 00:29:40,680
Inside a pellet of uranium, there would be billions and billions of atoms.

358
00:29:41,120 --> 00:29:44,620
So in very short order, we can get a chain reaction going in the uranium.

359
00:29:44,980 --> 00:29:47,440
So when you have a billion atoms releasing a little bit of heat,

360
00:29:47,480 --> 00:29:49,100
it becomes a large amount of heat.

361
00:29:49,820 --> 00:29:52,660
This heat is released inside the nuclear reactor,

362
00:29:52,900 --> 00:29:54,900
which is housed inside a containment dome.

363
00:29:54,901 --> 00:30:00,500
The walls are four and a half feet thick concrete and steel, and in theory would be able to

364
00:30:00,501 --> 00:30:04,040
survive any man-made or natural disaster, earthquakes.

365
00:30:04,041 --> 00:30:13,180
In this stress test, the wall withstood a direct impact of an F-4 Phantom jet.

366
00:30:13,181 --> 00:30:17,180
The reactor and its circulating system are filled with water, which submerges the fuel

367
00:30:17,181 --> 00:30:18,180
core.

368
00:30:18,181 --> 00:30:23,500
If you were to put a thermometer on the core, the fuel assembly itself would be in excess

369
00:30:23,501 --> 00:30:24,530
of 600 degrees.

370
00:30:24,531 --> 00:30:29,540
As the water circulates by that hot uranium, the water gets hot, it comes

371
00:30:29,541 --> 00:30:35,159
out the other side, and it goes into two very large devices called steam generators.

372
00:30:35,160 --> 00:30:35,860
The

373
00:30:35,861 --> 00:30:40,160
steam drives the mechanical turbine, causes it to rotate at 1,800 revolutions per minute.

374
00:30:40,680 --> 00:30:45,560
That in turn turns an electric generator, and electrons are forced through wires making

375
00:30:45,561 --> 00:30:48,660
electricity to do power to run homes and businesses.

376
00:30:48,661 --> 00:30:58,440
The row of turbines and the generator together weigh in at 800 tons, or the equivalent of 800 Volkswagen Beetles.

377
00:30:58,960 --> 00:31:02,980
But despite this massive scale, it's still a fundamental process.

378
00:31:04,060 --> 00:31:09,400
As radioactive water from the reactor flows through the steam generators in a closed system,

379
00:31:09,840 --> 00:31:14,020
it heats clean water in a second closed system before it returns to the reactor.

380
00:31:14,021 --> 00:31:20,980
The clean steam drives the turbine and is then cooled by ocean water before returning to the steam generator,

381
00:31:21,480 --> 00:31:23,940
where it will again be turned into steam.

382
00:31:26,000 --> 00:31:28,640
Behind me over here obviously is the Pacific Ocean.

383
00:31:29,220 --> 00:31:35,480
And about 3,200 feet offshore, we have a 16-foot in diameter pipe that's in the ocean.

384
00:31:36,080 --> 00:31:41,860
And when we turn on the pumps, we draw in about 1.6 million gallons of water per minute.

385
00:31:41,861 --> 00:31:46,860
The pumps are run by 4,000 horsepower motors.

386
00:31:46,861 --> 00:31:50,860
That's the equivalent of six NASCAR cars running at full speed.

387
00:31:50,861 --> 00:31:55,860
Each pump is capable of draining an Olympic swimming pool in 15 seconds.

388
00:31:55,861 --> 00:31:59,860
If for any reason engineers needed to stop a nuclear reaction,

389
00:31:59,861 --> 00:32:03,860
they could do so by lowering an array of control rods into the core.

390
00:32:03,861 --> 00:32:06,860
The control rods absorb the atom-splitting neutrons

391
00:32:06,861 --> 00:32:09,860
that are driving the chain reaction, bringing it to a halt.

392
00:32:09,861 --> 00:32:16,860
But the surprising aspect of this plant is that, to a great degree, it runs without stopping.

393
00:32:16,861 --> 00:32:21,860
It can run for two years without needing new fuel at full output.

394
00:32:21,861 --> 00:32:26,860
San Onofre Unit Number 2 has been operating for about 530 days consecutively.

395
00:32:26,861 --> 00:32:30,860
If this was a natural gas plant or an oil plant or a coal plant,

396
00:32:30,861 --> 00:32:36,860
we'd constantly be needing to bring in trains or pipelines bringing in this fuel product.

397
00:32:39,860 --> 00:32:46,360
Outside, transformers step 22,000 volts of electricity up to 220,000 volts,

398
00:32:46,860 --> 00:32:50,240
essentially pressurizing it for transmission throughout Southern California.

399
00:32:51,120 --> 00:32:55,620
Although nuclear power is a relatively new and seemingly successful technology,

400
00:32:56,100 --> 00:32:59,740
it has yet to recover from an accident in Harrisburg, Pennsylvania.

401
00:33:00,300 --> 00:33:05,317
The most significant event from a safety standpoint that has ever happened in the U.S.

402
00:33:05,318 --> 00:33:06,600
nuclear power industry

403
00:33:06,601 --> 00:33:09,340
was the Three Mile Island accident in 1979.

404
00:33:09,860 --> 00:33:13,160
This was literally a brand new reactor with brand new operators.

405
00:33:13,320 --> 00:33:18,300
Four o'clock in the morning, because of a combination of human error and mechanical design,

406
00:33:18,880 --> 00:33:24,200
they allowed the reactor's core, the uranium, to be uncovered for several hours.

407
00:33:24,620 --> 00:33:29,060
Without any water to keep the uranium cool, the uranium overheated and actually melted.

408
00:33:30,940 --> 00:33:34,240
Evidence suggests that fallout from the accident was low.

409
00:33:34,740 --> 00:33:37,420
But the public relations setback was immense.

410
00:33:38,000 --> 00:33:40,140
No new plants have been built since.

411
00:33:41,880 --> 00:33:46,620
However, most nuclear power advocates predict that the industry will stage a comeback.

412
00:33:47,480 --> 00:33:49,860
In my opinion, there will be a rebirth of nuclear power.

413
00:33:49,980 --> 00:33:51,800
It's not a question of if, it's a question of when.

414
00:33:52,400 --> 00:33:56,740
And proponents suggest the question of what kind of waste we want in our environment

415
00:33:56,741 --> 00:33:59,820
may well be nuclear power's most convincing argument.

416
00:34:00,600 --> 00:34:06,340
Nuclear power in the United States supplies about 20% of the electricity in the whole United States.

417
00:34:06,341 --> 00:34:09,480
that would probably surprise most people that it's that high.

418
00:34:10,220 --> 00:34:14,580
In that 20%, that avoids, in a yearly basis,

419
00:34:14,581 --> 00:34:21,580
the emission of about 177 million metric tons of greenhouse gases

420
00:34:21,581 --> 00:34:23,780
that would be emitted into the atmosphere

421
00:34:23,781 --> 00:34:28,600
if that 20% nuclear power was replaced, say, with coal or oil.

422
00:34:28,800 --> 00:34:31,200
I would say that in the next 10 to 20 years,

423
00:34:31,540 --> 00:34:35,400
you will see the percentage of nuclear power rising

424
00:34:35,401 --> 00:34:39,000
because that's a good way to address the global warming issue

425
00:34:39,001 --> 00:34:45,580
and you do not have any solid waste other than the radioactive waste that you have to deal with.

426
00:34:45,680 --> 00:34:48,860
But I feel like we'll figure out a way to manage that problem.

427
00:34:50,180 --> 00:34:50,880
Perhaps.

428
00:34:51,300 --> 00:34:56,660
But the 3,000 tons of spent fuel that the industry produces each year has no permanent home

429
00:34:56,661 --> 00:35:00,300
and is simply being held at the nuclear plants that produced it.

430
00:35:00,940 --> 00:35:04,160
Since this waste will remain radioactive for thousands of years,

431
00:35:04,161 --> 00:35:09,340
It's not surprising that most elected officials continue to say, not in my backyard.

432
00:35:10,480 --> 00:35:15,380
Next, electrical consumption grows as interest in conservation wanes.

433
00:35:16,100 --> 00:35:18,380
How will power plants of the future meet the demand?

434
00:35:19,680 --> 00:35:23,240
All natural substances contain radioactive material.

435
00:35:23,880 --> 00:35:30,780
In fact, beer contains 13 times as much radioactivity as the cooling water discharged from a nuclear power plant.

436
00:35:31,640 --> 00:35:34,260
Power plants will return on Modern Marvels.

437
00:35:36,200 --> 00:35:41,220
Although the history of power plants is largely one of growth and technological triumph,

438
00:35:41,820 --> 00:35:45,360
the future of electrical generation could not be more uncertain.

439
00:35:46,040 --> 00:35:48,600
What is clear is that we have come to a crossroads

440
00:35:48,601 --> 00:35:54,080
where environmental concerns, unchecked consumption, and technological limits meet.

441
00:35:54,700 --> 00:35:58,720
The size of a power plant really has capped out at about 1,000 megawatts.

442
00:35:58,721 --> 00:36:02,060
and I was thinking about why that's really the limit.

443
00:36:02,580 --> 00:36:06,100
The boiler in a coal facility right now is the size of an apartment building.

444
00:36:06,660 --> 00:36:08,040
It's 11 stories high.

445
00:36:08,840 --> 00:36:13,420
Right now you've got turbine blades that in some cases are 14 feet long.

446
00:36:14,180 --> 00:36:22,020
The tip speed of something 14 feet long rotating at 3,600 RPM starts to get supersonic.

447
00:36:23,200 --> 00:36:28,160
Today, the country's electrical needs are supplied by regional interconnected power grids.

448
00:36:28,720 --> 00:36:31,900
that in theory ensure that power flows where and when it is needed.

449
00:36:32,420 --> 00:36:35,640
In practice, these grids are straining to meet demand.

450
00:36:37,260 --> 00:36:41,440
It's really a series of regional grids at this point.

451
00:36:41,580 --> 00:36:48,020
I mean, you know, theoretically, you can move power from Texas to California.

452
00:36:48,740 --> 00:36:52,400
However, there might only be one really good path to do that.

453
00:36:52,401 --> 00:36:56,820
But ideally, you would want to have more options than that.

454
00:36:56,821 --> 00:37:05,280
The lack of options in routing electricity is further complicated by heavy demand in summertime that is beginning to cause power shortages.

455
00:37:05,800 --> 00:37:07,042
It's an issue of generation.

456
00:37:07,043 --> 00:37:09,880
It's an issue of not having enough kilowatt hours in the system.

457
00:37:10,460 --> 00:37:17,800
And it's occasioned by catastrophic failures at a facility or even small problems that take it offline for several hours.

458
00:37:18,280 --> 00:37:24,240
The flip side of insufficient power generation is a countrywide lack of interest in energy conservation.

459
00:37:24,241 --> 00:37:32,940
I see probably less emphasis and care about energy efficiency today than I ever have.

460
00:37:33,620 --> 00:37:41,040
I can't even get people that I know and whatever to turn off their lights when they don't use it.

461
00:37:41,100 --> 00:37:47,820
Right here in my office here, I can't switch off the lights in my own office or in individual rooms.

462
00:37:48,640 --> 00:37:52,560
Over a third of the world's population doesn't have electricity today.

463
00:37:53,260 --> 00:37:55,840
And there aren't people looking to not have electricity,

464
00:37:56,120 --> 00:38:00,120
so it's always more load, more load coming on globally.

465
00:38:00,640 --> 00:38:04,700
Well, we've got to ask ourselves as a society or as creatures of the earth

466
00:38:04,701 --> 00:38:09,380
how we're going to give that load, and it isn't going to be burning fossil fuels.

467
00:38:09,520 --> 00:38:12,300
When you talk about the environmental implications of power creation,

468
00:38:12,740 --> 00:38:15,420
you first have to look at the type of primary fuel.

469
00:38:15,960 --> 00:38:20,680
I mean, if you're talking about oil, you're dealing with NOx or nitrous oxides,

470
00:38:20,681 --> 00:38:22,840
you're dealing with metals that are in the oil.

471
00:38:23,400 --> 00:38:25,200
And when you deal with coal, of course,

472
00:38:25,260 --> 00:38:26,800
then you're dealing with particulate emissions,

473
00:38:27,060 --> 00:38:29,880
in some cases trace elements of heavy metals,

474
00:38:30,100 --> 00:38:31,940
and you're dealing with particulate emissions,

475
00:38:32,080 --> 00:38:34,820
which is almost a dust that comes out of the coal.

476
00:38:35,360 --> 00:38:38,860
The immediate health risks of heavy metals and particulate emissions

477
00:38:38,861 --> 00:38:41,800
pale in comparison to the global implications

478
00:38:41,801 --> 00:38:44,600
of increased carbon dioxide in our atmosphere.

479
00:38:45,560 --> 00:38:49,320
Carbon dioxide, which is a direct result of burning fossil fuels.

480
00:38:50,320 --> 00:38:54,420
Concentrations of this greenhouse gas have increased sharply in the upper atmosphere

481
00:38:54,421 --> 00:38:56,680
since the beginning of the Industrial Revolution,

482
00:38:57,340 --> 00:39:01,040
warming our environment and threatening catastrophic climate change.

483
00:39:01,680 --> 00:39:04,020
In the face of a deteriorating environment,

484
00:39:04,580 --> 00:39:07,180
we clearly need to exploit a clean energy source.

485
00:39:07,540 --> 00:39:08,300
But which one?

486
00:39:09,080 --> 00:39:12,560
The Earth's core is much hotter than the Earth's surface, fortunately.

487
00:39:13,420 --> 00:39:17,180
And the interaction of these temperature gradients

488
00:39:17,181 --> 00:39:20,380
comes very close to the surface in many parts of the world,

489
00:39:20,840 --> 00:39:25,260
and that can be used to create a hot fluid or create steam,

490
00:39:25,400 --> 00:39:27,000
which then drives conventional turbines.

491
00:39:27,840 --> 00:39:29,780
At a geothermal plant,

492
00:39:30,120 --> 00:39:32,960
wells are drilled hundreds of feet below the Earth's surface

493
00:39:32,961 --> 00:39:37,580
into reservoirs with temperatures of 180 degrees Celsius or more.

494
00:39:38,500 --> 00:39:40,640
Although Mother Nature supplies the heat,

495
00:39:41,220 --> 00:39:46,020
geothermal sources like geysers are neither widespread nor are they clean.

496
00:39:46,021 --> 00:39:48,920
Fraud with problems in terms of first, it's tough to locate.

497
00:39:49,180 --> 00:39:52,320
There is none in the Northeast that I'm aware of, so we have difficulty using it here.

498
00:39:52,800 --> 00:39:56,960
With a geothermal facility, your primary pollutant is hydrogen sulfide.

499
00:39:56,961 --> 00:40:02,640
So the problem there is taking the hydrogen sulfide out, capturing it,

500
00:40:02,720 --> 00:40:06,320
and then finding some way to deal with that once you've captured it.

501
00:40:06,860 --> 00:40:09,940
The most futuristic of technologies, fusion,

502
00:40:10,560 --> 00:40:14,800
combines two light atomic nuclei to form a single heavier nucleus.

503
00:40:15,640 --> 00:40:19,680
This generates heat levels similar to the plasma state of the sun.

504
00:40:20,120 --> 00:40:28,700
That's a dream for egomaniac scientists who figure that they can take the absolute most cataclysmic forces that could be known to man and manage them.

505
00:40:29,760 --> 00:40:34,800
There is, however, a less dramatic way to capture the energy of the sun, solar power.

506
00:40:35,400 --> 00:40:38,740
But the debate surrounding it is about efficiency, not safety.

507
00:40:38,741 --> 00:40:43,740
Even in areas of the country where the sun shines a lot, like in the desert southwest,

508
00:40:43,741 --> 00:40:51,740
the technology is still very behind being able to supply the big base load plants like coal or nuclear plants.

509
00:40:51,741 --> 00:41:00,740
For an example, to replace an average nuclear plant would take about 600 square miles of solar cells with today's technology.

510
00:41:00,741 --> 00:41:04,740
Solar advocates say that it is not so much the technology,

511
00:41:04,741 --> 00:41:09,400
but the concept of massive centralized power plants that limit solar's effectiveness.

512
00:41:10,640 --> 00:41:14,340
Perhaps the power plant of the future looks something like this,

513
00:41:15,100 --> 00:41:19,280
a house on the main coast that generates more energy than it consumes.

514
00:41:19,680 --> 00:41:24,940
This house incorporates both solar thermal for space heating and hot water

515
00:41:24,941 --> 00:41:29,320
and solar electricity for electrical production.

516
00:41:29,321 --> 00:41:35,960
The panels are integrated to form the finished weathering skin of the south roof

517
00:41:35,961 --> 00:41:38,260
And the house pretty much exists

518
00:41:38,261 --> 00:41:43,980
In terms of its heating, hot water and electrical energy requirements

519
00:41:43,981 --> 00:41:48,240
From the harvested energy that falls on the roof

520
00:41:48,241 --> 00:41:51,960
The house does more than just exist

521
00:41:51,961 --> 00:41:54,960
It contributes to the energy needs of its community

522
00:41:54,961 --> 00:41:58,740
And homeowner William Lord is quite proud of this fact

523
00:41:58,741 --> 00:42:02,200
We make most of our own hot water and all of our electricity.

524
00:42:02,420 --> 00:42:06,480
In fact, we generate more electricity than we actually use.

525
00:42:07,300 --> 00:42:13,240
So the process of fitting into the environment is seamless in our case and is not disruptive.

526
00:42:14,200 --> 00:42:19,000
Solar power not only makes environmental sense, but economic sense as well.

527
00:42:19,500 --> 00:42:20,840
We have two meters on the house.

528
00:42:21,320 --> 00:42:24,760
One measures the excess energy that we export to the power company,

529
00:42:24,761 --> 00:42:28,960
and the other meter measures what energy we take from the power company.

530
00:42:29,240 --> 00:42:31,740
And in Maine we have something called annualized net metering

531
00:42:31,741 --> 00:42:37,340
and the bottom line is that we essentially pay no electric bill annually.

532
00:42:38,260 --> 00:42:42,240
When we built our solar house we didn't realize we were actually creating an event.

533
00:42:43,160 --> 00:42:47,420
We added to that event a place where people come to learn about solar power.

534
00:42:47,580 --> 00:42:48,680
We have many visitors here.

535
00:42:49,080 --> 00:42:51,340
We added to that event our website.

536
00:42:52,160 --> 00:42:55,980
The main solar house gets more than one million hits a year.

537
00:42:56,780 --> 00:42:59,660
This month I'm taking a look at a house in County Bunkport nearby.

538
00:43:00,600 --> 00:43:06,340
The former president of the United States, George Bush, has solar thermal panels on his roof.

539
00:43:06,341 --> 00:43:11,460
I think that's a great hope for everybody because clearly that is probably the best way to produce power.

540
00:43:12,040 --> 00:43:15,240
Taking incidents, solar energy doesn't pollute anything.

541
00:43:15,241 --> 00:43:24,860
The direct conversion of solar energy into electricity with photovoltaics is the most environmentally benign method of making electricity.

542
00:43:26,180 --> 00:43:33,880
And so that coupled with other conversion measures such as wind energy conversion, hydropower,

543
00:43:33,881 --> 00:43:45,200
can combine to gradually displace the conventional systems to build a resilient energy economy based on renewables.

544
00:43:45,240 --> 00:43:48,240
It's not a question of if, but when.

545
00:43:48,241 --> 00:43:51,240
And from my perspective, time is short.

546
00:43:51,241 --> 00:43:57,640
We should be investing the relatively plentiful and relatively inexpensive reserves of conventional

547
00:43:57,641 --> 00:44:03,240
energy to build this technology bridge to the future, because these are finite resources

548
00:44:03,241 --> 00:44:06,240
and they are not going to be here indefinitely.

